1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:04,813
How can quadrupeds avoid moving obstacles despite delayed commands?

2
00:00:04,813 --> 00:00:09,549
VAC tackles this in simulation and on the real robot.

3
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:16,444
Two tracks propose exploration and braking.
The Safe-Time Contract revalidates timing;

4
00:00:16,444 --> 00:00:19,659
our velocity filter adjusts the command.

5
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:29,721
Align the obstacle track with control time. The circle
combines both bodies, margins, and uncertainty.

6
00:00:29,721 --> 00:00:33,067
Braking must fit within the remaining gap.

7
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:37,190
How much clearance does a delayed command need?

8
00:00:37,190 --> 00:00:42,045
We can see the answer by linking motion to the area under a speed curve.

9
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:49,936
Here, closing speed stays constant during the
delay. The rectangle measures the distance lost.

10
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:56,472
Braking adds a triangle. Its base is the
stopping time; its height is the closing speed.

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,136
Adding the two areas gives the required clearance.

12
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:08,940
Solving for closing speed turns this distance
budget into an admissible speed limit.

13
00:01:13,550 --> 00:01:17,311
Each obstacle defines a half-plane in velocity space.

14
00:01:17,311 --> 00:01:25,302
We project the requested velocity onto their intersection
with actuator limits. When only one plane is active,

15
00:01:25,302 --> 00:01:27,182
lateral motion is preserved.

16
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:35,833
We require a feasible relative-braking backup and full execution coverage.

17
00:01:35,833 --> 00:01:39,531
These conditions are not yet certified online.

18
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:47,229
All 10 methods share a 30 × 10 m dynamic scene. Replays run at 6× speed,

19
00:01:47,229 --> 00:01:50,344
with the start below and the goal above.

20
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:03,324
Final results show static and dynamic
collisions, goal arrival, and run time in seconds.

21
00:02:03,324 --> 00:02:08,032
FAR times out at 180 seconds. Aggregate performance follows.

22
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:20,300
VAC reaches 87.9% collision-free arrivals:
5.0 percentage points above delay-aware CBF.

23
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:26,550
The filter's 95th-percentile computation time is 8.9 ms.

24
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:31,412
The outdoor Go2 demonstration plays at original speed.

25
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:43,871
Over 30 trials per method, VAC records
93.3% success and 3.3% manual takeover,

26
00:02:43,871 --> 00:02:47,139
the best observed rates among 10 methods.

27
00:02:52,350 --> 00:02:59,450
VAC combines temporal planning and velocity filtering
for quadruped navigation in dynamic environments.

